{"id": "De_2zoOznsh", "title": "Video by therishikatha", "description": "How many lines are on YOUR rudraksha? 🔢 The Shiva Purana gives each number its own god. Count yours before you read on 👇\n\nRudraksha means “the eye of Rudra”. In the Shiva Purana (Vidyeshvara Samhita, ch. 25), Shiva says he sat in tapas for thousands of divine years. When he opened his eyes, tears fell to the earth, and from those tears grew the rudraksha trees.\n\nThe bead is the seed of a BLUE fruit (the blue isn’t a pigment: it’s structural colour, like a peacock’s feather).\n\nNow count the lines (mukhi) running from top to bottom:\n1 – Shiva himself\n2 – Isha, lord of the gods\n3 – brings all learning\n4 – Brahma\n5 – Kalagni Rudra\n6 – Kartikeya\n7 – Ananga (Kamadeva)\n8 – Bhairava\n9 – Durga, in her nine forms\n10 – Vishnu (Janardana)\n11 – Rudra\n12 – the twelve Adityas, the sun gods\n13 – the Vishvedevas\n14 – Parama Shiva, the highest\n\nAlmost every rudraksha (about 99%) has FIVE lines, so most of us are already wearing Kalagni Rudra. And the Purana says a mantra chanted on rudraksha is a crore times more powerful (25.58).\n\n📚 Want your kids to grow up knowing our gods and their stories? Our picture books Books 1–3 are now a set of 3 for ₹500 at rishikatha.com (link in bio).\n\n💾 SAVE this list for your next puja, SHARE it with someone who wears a rudraksha, and COMMENT the number of lines on yours!\n\n#rudraksha #rudrakshamala #mahadev #shiva #harharmahadev shivapurana bholenath omnamahshivaya sanatandharma hinduism mahakal kartikeya rishikatha", "channel": "therishikatha", "uploader_id": "22569163881", "uploader": "Rishi Katha", "timestamp": 1793104157, "like_count": 23129, "comment_count": 88, "http_headers": {"Referer": "https://www.instagram.com/"}, "formats": [{"format_id": "dash-1083946997786335a", "ext": "m4a", "tbr": 58.27, "asr": 44100, "format_note": "DASH audio", "container": "m4a_dash", "vcodec": "none", "acodec": "mp4a.40.5", "url": "https://instagram.fblr22-2.fna.fbc